WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.380
This video is brought to you by Wix.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.900
Street art has captured the world’s attention
in recent years, developing its very own graffiti

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.420
industrial complex.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.029
Skater kid Shepard Fairey parlayed his 80s
sticker street art into a guerilla marketing

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:17.920
company.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.500
He’s now worth millions.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.920
90s graffiti artist Retna turned his scribbles
into a cool multi-million dollars worth of

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.929
fine art.

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:31.900
But the Coca-Cola of this market, undoubtedly,
is Banksy, the anonymous dude with that voice…

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.360
The guy built a fake, depressing Disney World,
made a debatably fake, amusing documentary

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.770
and shredded his painting right after it sold
for massive amounts of money.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.560
He also bought a painting at a thrift shop
for $50, painted Hitler into it, and sold

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.200
it for charity for hundreds of thousands dollars.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.020
People tend to think that Banksy is either
some asshole worthy only of parody,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.240
“That’s when I told Lady Gaga, ‘No,
YOU text ME!”...

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:01.700
She texted me”

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.820
Or that he’s an artistic genius.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:08.960
The real question is: What is Banksy trying
to do with his art, and is his work achieving

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.020
its intended goal?

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.520
We’re going to find out in this Wisecrack
edition on Banksy - Deep, or Dumb?

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:20.830
But, before we get to it - a message from
me, Mark AKA Garyx Wormuloid, about this video’s

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:21.980
sponsor Wix.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.420
As an actor in LA it can be very hard to set
yourself apart, which is why I decided to

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.390
use Wix to make a professional website that I can shamelessly send out to Hollywood producers.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.130
As you can see here, I went on there and I
just told them that I’ve never created a

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.990
website before, and it walked me through the
process step by step!

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.909
Within minutes I was uploading all my photos,
all my videos, and some Earthling Cinema highlights

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:41.270
- it was super easy.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:45.420
So if you wanna step up your professional
game, head to wix.com/wisecrack or click the

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.860
link in the video description.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.440
Garyx out.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.080
Arguably the world’s most famous street
artist, Banksy is a professional prankster

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.330
best known for making satirical graffiti that
frequently depicts children, rats, and repurposed

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.330
iconography.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.390
Typically, the images will be accompanied
by a slogan, and the various facets will combine

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:06.920
to form some sort of anti-war, anti-capitalist,
or anti-establishment message.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.880
Of course, Banksy isn’t your typical street
artist - Even by art world standards, he’s

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.770
made a lot of money.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.560
His estimated worth is over 20 million dollars.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.120
What’s more, his art is no longer typical
street art - it’s frequently safeguarded

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.820
with plexiglass or removed from buildings
entirely to be protected for posterity.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.260
Cool, but is it deep, or dumb?

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.440
Argument 1 - Yes.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.800
Banksy is v. smart - the latest iteration
of a long-line of smart art people doing smart

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.920
art things.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.020
Because art is often appraised and understood
by its relation to other art, we need to look

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.510
back to Dadaism.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.660
Dadaism was a form of art that began in World
War I as a means of rejecting capitalism and

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.170
ridiculing the absurdity of modern life.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:54.010
It manifested as form of protest art, and
one of its central practitioners was Marcel

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:58.510
Duchamp, who famously coined the term “anti
art” to describe work that challenges our

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.460
preconceived definitions of what art should
be.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.680
Like, when he submitted a urinal on a pedestal,
titled “Fountain,” for an exhibition in

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:06.540
New York.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.140
For this, he used the term “ready made,”
to mean an already-produced or “found object”

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.280
that is repurposed as art.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.050
He chose mass-produced commercial objects,
typically ones that served a utilitarian purpose

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.000
- like, say, a urinal.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.880
As he said:

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.000
“You have to approach the object as if you
had no aesthetic emotion.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.420
The choice of ready-mades is always based
on visual indifference”

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:33.430
Duchamp was hoping that, by exhibiting something
that was explicitly NOT art, literally, anti-art,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.530
he could ironically call into question where
our concept of art starts and ends, while

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.069
also arguing that anything, even a urinal,
could be art.

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:45.890
Of his readymades, he also said, “I don’t
attach any value—I mean commercial value,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.040
or even artistic value—to it.”

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.810
So additionally, by displaying art that is
for practical purposes worthless, he is perhaps

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.050
questioning why we even value art in the first
place.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:56.880
Cool.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.840
For Duchamp, the “creative act” of art
was not the sole endeavour of the artist,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.080
but a sort of collaboration with the spectator,
who contributes to the creative act by “deciphering

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.269
and interpreting it”.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:11.970
With Duchamp, the reaction of his audience
was an essential part of the artwork itself.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:16.239
While Duchamp never admitted exactly what
he hoped people’s reactions would be to his

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.600
pedestaled piss pot, he does seem to have
expressed regret at how people actually reacted.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.820
Later in life, he bemoaned that “you can
make people swallow anything; that’s what

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.630
happened [with Fountain]” Duchamp seems
to be saying that, in attempting to challenge

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.880
the entire concept of “art”, he ended
up merely expanding the range of what kinds

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.320
of images and objects people would choose
to commodify and revere.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.590
Basically, Duchamp wanted to make you scrunch
up your nose and picture your “art” covered

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.320
in piss, as a productive means of challenging
conventional notions of what art should or

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.220
shouldn’t be.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.060
But instead of being grossed out, people were
like “Sweet, can I buy this used urinal

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.740
for 20 million dollars please?”

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.040
Which brings us to Banksy, who, like Duchamp,
critiques the pretentiousness of the art world.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:03.040
Banksy argues that “Art is not like other
culture because its success is not made by

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.080
its audience.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.500
The public fill concert halls and cinemas
everyday, we read novels by the millions,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.080
and buy records by the billions.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:16.060
'We the people' affect the making and quality
of most of our culture, but not our art.”

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.210
Like Duchamp, Banksy wants to “degrade”
the perceived snobbishness of the art world

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:26.440
by a) Using the everyday streets as his canvas
and b) Using familiar, simple images that

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.370
are universally accessible, regardless of
whether you just wrote your dissertation on

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:31.640
early Russian modernism.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.420
Furthermore, Banksy’s work is all about
criticizing capitalism and consumerism.

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:41.420
Ironically, the popularity of his work triggers
rabid consumerism, being physically removed

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.300
from buildings to be sold in auctions on the
art market, or protected from the elements

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.480
with plexiglass.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.460
Arguably, Banksy’s most Dada moment came
this past October when his painting “Girl

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:56.400
With Balloon“ self-destructed immediately
after being sold for just over a million pounds.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.460
Banksy was following a specific tradition
of auto-destructive art invented by artist

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.100
Gustav Metzger in the early 1960’s.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.880
Metzger famously used acid to paint a white
nylon sheet, in the process destroying it.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.419
Auto-destructive art emphasized the audience’s
participation in the work, with artists typically

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.000
destroying their work in a public space.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.620
The destruction, and bearing witness to that
destruction, was all part of the work.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:25.310
But he was also referencing the tradition
of Dadaism, as that ironically “degrades”

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.580
or destroys the bubble of the art world.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:32.630
In that way, Banksy’s latest work definitely
qualifies, by emphasizing the material fragility,

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.920
an ease with which “art” can be transformed
into a pile of strips.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:45.710
Even if you’re not a Banksy enthusiast,
you can probably admit that it was pretty

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:46.880
punk rock.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.540
More on balloon girl later.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.300
But don’t worry Banksy skeptics.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.860
There’s plenty here for you.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:56.700
Argument 2: Banksy is irrelevant, his audience’s
opinion is all that matters.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:02.050
According to the theory of structuralism articulated
by scholar Roland Barthes in his earlier years,

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.840
there is no foundation of meaning in an artist’s
intention.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.509
Rather, meaning is grounded in whatever culture
and systems exist at any given time.

00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:15.700
For example, the first caveman art critic
who looked at this ox was probably like “Daniel’s

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:20.520
really pushing his work to the next level,”
whereas we’re like “oh, cute finger-painting”.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:25.069
Barthes, as he wrote in his essay “Death
of the Author” sees meaning as being anchored

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:29.520
in the culture surrounding art, and it’s
our job as cultural detectives to figure out

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.640
what the eff this means.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:35.880
Structuralism makes sense when it comes to
an artist like Banksy, who purposely remains

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:39.449
anonymous and gives evasive answers about
his intentions.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:44.610
By not declaring exactly what any one piece
of art means, Banksy is protecting our ability

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.690
to decide for ourselves, thus giving the audience
all the interpretive space in the world to

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:55.440
theorize and argue over answers we’ll never
have, such as whether or not his “documentary,”

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.509
Exit Through the Gift Shop is fake.

00:07:57.509 --> 00:08:02.550
For an example of work that relies almost
entirely on societal context to be understood,

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.400
look at Banksy’s Kissing Coppers.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.840
In the image two male police officers passionately
kiss.

00:08:07.849 --> 00:08:12.819
This work was widely viewed as a repudiation
of homophobia, a middle finger to the ugly

00:08:12.819 --> 00:08:16.580
tradition of the law telling people who and
how they should love.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.990
However, compare Kissing Coppers to now-routine
images of President Trump and Vladimir Putin

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:25.110
engaged in various forms of romantic intimacy.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:29.449
These images are clearly disparaging, suggesting
that Trump’s perceived subordination to

00:08:29.449 --> 00:08:33.990
Putin is embarrassing or shameful through
the metaphor of gay intimacy.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.349
In other words, if Banksy’s anonymity is
meant, smartly, to give more power to the

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:43.569
spectator and their interpretation, is that
completely ruined if half of the people read

00:08:43.569 --> 00:08:46.379
your work in the opposite way you intended?

00:08:46.379 --> 00:08:51.300
In the case of Kissing Coppers, did he set
out to mock homophobia, or was he merely trying

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:56.940
to humiliate cops by suggesting that these
stereotypical manly men harbor gay fantasies?

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.200
Does this ambiguity subvert Banksy’s ability
to accomplish anything?

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.720
Or does it unapologetically function as a
mirror to its viewer - who are free to interpret

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.139
it as either pro gay rights or simply homophobic?

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.240
Does his anonymity hinder his ability to communicate
his message?

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:18.639
Argument 3: There was never any chance for
Banksy to be deep because postmodernism.

00:09:18.639 --> 00:09:23.100
For an even less flattering angle on Banksy,
let’s look to Thomas Mann’s postmodernist

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:27.429
theories, which hold that there’s basically
no way for a successful artist to critique

00:09:27.429 --> 00:09:29.100
the art market through art.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:29.800
Why?

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:34.379
Well, because the second they start making
art that is recognized by the commercial market,

00:09:34.379 --> 00:09:36.670
they have thus entered said market.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.240
So are all successful artists sellouts?

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:44.579
Would a Banksy, writhing around, broke, in
obscurity, tagging the streets of London be

00:09:44.579 --> 00:09:47.170
more effective than rolling-in-dough Banksy?

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:48.420
Well, it’s possible.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.880
That said, only rolling-in-dough Banksy could
pull off a project like Dismaland, a temporary

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:59.040
art project and “anti-amusement park”
that featured works from 58 other artists.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.480
It sold out almost instantly, with six million
people trying to buy tickets and crashing

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:05.340
the site.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.500
People waited for hours to gain entry.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.460
It’s been criticized as something high-brow,
middle class millennials participated in to

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:13.799
feel better about themselves.

00:10:13.799 --> 00:10:19.899
Arguably, in setting out to critique the self-indulgent
branded fun of amusement parks, Banksy simply

00:10:19.899 --> 00:10:23.119
replicated that experience, with a snarky
facade.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.700
If he set out to create an anti-amusement
park, he may have simply just made a hipster

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:28.900
Disneyworld.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:33.879
Also, the project was criticized as not really
having anything to say besides “capitalism

00:10:33.879 --> 00:10:34.879
bad.”

00:10:34.879 --> 00:10:37.299
Let’s briefly jump back to Girl with a Balloon.

00:10:37.299 --> 00:10:41.649
Here, Banksy pulled off a pretty cool stunt
that gave the middle finger to all the art

00:10:41.649 --> 00:10:43.380
snobs bidding on his work.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.940
However, after the piece was shredded, it
only increased its value.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:53.340
As Leon Benrimon, the director of modern and
contemporary art at Heritage Auctions explains,

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:58.820
“It was meant to be a criticism of the art
market, and I think it’s going to double

00:10:58.839 --> 00:11:00.449
the value of the work.”

00:11:00.449 --> 00:11:04.579
This is the ultimate example of the “damned
if he does, damned if he doesn’t” conundrum

00:11:04.579 --> 00:11:07.879
of being Banksy, or any other artist for that
matter.

00:11:07.879 --> 00:11:11.040
You’d almost have to feel bad for the guy,
if he wasn’t a millionaire.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.769
One last point on this - So Banksy’s anti
capitalist work has been incorporated into

00:11:15.769 --> 00:11:18.619
the very capitalist system he dislikes.

00:11:18.619 --> 00:11:21.259
Now people pay top dollar for his work:

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.460
“Selling for eight hundred and sixty thousand”

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.259
and retweet his art on the ad-soaked feeds
of Instagram.

00:11:26.259 --> 00:11:29.689
But what if this is just proof of Banksy’s
success?

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:35.910
Here we are, at Dismaland or Banksy’s bethlehem
hotel or gathering around a random gas station

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.860
or a hoity-toity art auction to consume his
work.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:44.679
What if us voraciously partaking in this system
- the very thing he’s making fun of - means

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:48.000
that the joke, quite simply, is on us?

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.660
On to Argument 4 - Banksy is deep because
he’s a culture jammer in the grand tradition

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:55.259
of Situationism.

00:11:55.259 --> 00:11:59.209
The Situationist International (SI) was an
organization of social revolutionaries made

00:11:59.209 --> 00:12:04.049
up of avant-garde artists, intellectuals,
and political theorists who ran around Europe

00:12:04.049 --> 00:12:07.900
pulling artsy lolz from 1957-1972.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:12.420
One of the group’s favorite tactics was
the Situationist Prank, a subversive political

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.240
prank, hoax or stunt.

00:12:14.249 --> 00:12:18.720
For instance, when some Situationists dressed
up like monks, and gave a sermon on Easter

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:23.910
Sunday at Notre Dame announcing “Verily
I say unto you: God is dead.”

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:29.379
They used a technique called ‘détournement’,
French for ‘rerouting’ or ‘hijacking’,

00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:33.189
which essentially means changing something
so that it means something entirely different

00:12:33.189 --> 00:12:37.660
than its original intention, particularly
in regards to capitalist messaging.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:42.960
Like if I write “Capitalism does not suck,”
on my store window, and you crossed out the

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.360
“not”, that would be a very dumb example
of detournement and nobody would be impressed.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:53.259
Or say, painting blood on the Nike logo because,
you know, sweatshops.

00:12:53.259 --> 00:12:58.239
Culture jamming, taking its influence from
the Situationists, tries to disrupt our environments,

00:12:58.239 --> 00:13:01.529
which are so deeply saturated by corporate
and consumer imagery.

00:13:01.529 --> 00:13:07.910
“The average North American consumes five
times more than a Mexican, ten times more

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:12.759
than a Chinese person, and thirty times more
than a person from India”

00:13:12.759 --> 00:13:18.220
They do so by using mainstream language and
rhetoric subversively to critique the culture

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.320
and force us to reconsider it.

00:13:20.589 --> 00:13:25.460
We definitely see Banksy interrogate these
questions in works like Slave Labor, which

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:30.399
depicts a young boy making Union Jack bunting
ahead of the Diamond Jubilee in 2013.

00:13:30.399 --> 00:13:34.810
A pretty straightforward critique of sweatshop
labor, the image was painted on the exterior

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.329
of a Poundland discount store in London.

00:13:37.329 --> 00:13:43.639
However, it was removed, framed and sold in
an auction for over 700 thousand pounds.

00:13:43.639 --> 00:13:48.439
Ironically, what started as a provocative
stunt ended up being co-opted by the very

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:52.360
market forces Banksy was critiquing in the
first place.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:53.980
Successful detournement?

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.160
Maybe not.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:00.199
Compare his efforts to those of The Yes Men,
led by culture-jamming duo Jacques Servin

00:14:00.199 --> 00:14:04.439
and Igor Vamos, operating under the mission
of using “humor and trickery to highlight

00:14:04.439 --> 00:14:06.119
the corporate takeover of society”,

00:14:06.119 --> 00:14:10.679
“I think Barry as well as all the other
protesters are simply, in a word, focused

00:14:10.679 --> 00:14:13.709
too much on reality and on facts and figures”

00:14:13.709 --> 00:14:18.680
The Yes Men made a name for themselves creating spoof websites and posing as corporate spokesmen.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.240
In one example, they impersonated World Trade
Organization spokesman and pitched the idea

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.160
of using first-world waste to provide third-world
food.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:30.839
“The number one, number two, number three,
number four, number five would no longer refer

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:36.340
to combinations of food, but rather just uh
- the number of times the product had been

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:36.860
recycled”

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:40.680
This functioned as effective culture jamming
because, for one, the performance couldn’t

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:46.020
be directly commodified (though it would be
memorialized in a Yes Men documentary film)

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:51.040
and it directly confronts the powerful - fully
employing corporate lexicon in service of

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:56.560
constructing colorful, elaborate lies that
reveal stunning truths about the full extent

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:02.119
of corporate greed, while indicting the cold,
calculated, fundamentally dishonest nature

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.119
of business-speak.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:08.620
Instead, we might put Banksy alongside the
likes of fellow graffiti-artist Shepard Fairey,

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:13.560
whose Obey stickers might have once invoked
this anti-consumerist message in the film

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:17.460
“They Live” , but now adorns a $44 doormat.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:22.780
Or, Supreme, who took the anarchists’ beloved
brick, an object perfect for sending through

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:27.160
a bank window, and stamped their logo on it
for a limited edition collectors item.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:31.440
You can now buy one on ebay for over a thousand
dollars.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:32.460
Radical!

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.720
So at the end of the day, what should you
think of Banksy?

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.499
Well whatever the hell you want, obviously.

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:41.360
You can find his art valid and construe meaning
but is it productive?

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:42.380
Not really.

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.860
Will it incite a revolution to overthrow capitalism?

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:46.960
Definitely unlikely.

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:52.160
Is its massive popularity and high value in
the art market necessarily at odds with its

00:15:52.169 --> 00:15:55.389
success as anti-establishment intervention?

00:15:55.389 --> 00:15:56.389
Almost certainly.

00:15:56.389 --> 00:15:58.040
So what do you think?

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:01.239
Is Banksy deep, dumb, or somewhere in between?

00:16:01.239 --> 00:16:03.779
And is art really better when nobody knows
about it?

00:16:03.779 --> 00:16:07.209
Let us know in the comments, and if you wanna
build your own website with Wix, click the

00:16:07.209 --> 00:16:10.140
link in the description, and as always - peace!

